    "result_text": "The implementation is complete and the test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, including the `coreRejectsDuplicateEmail` acceptance test).\n\n## What I did\n\nAdded a single new class, `OwnerUniqueEmailAdvice`, at:\n`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/advice/OwnerUniqueEmailAdvice.java`\n\nIt mirrors the existing `OwnerUniqueTelephoneAdvice` pattern already established in this codebase for 409 rules: a `@ControllerAdvice` implementing `RequestBodyAdvice` that fires only on the `addOwner` create body, scans existing owners via `clinicService.findAllOwners()`, and \u2014 if the incoming lower-cased email matches an existing owner's \u2014 throws a private `DuplicateEmailException` handled locally into `409 Conflict`. Null/absent emails are skipped, and email is compared case-insensitively (matching `Owner.setEmail`, which stores it lower-cased).\n\n## Why this keeps structural impact low\n\n- **One file touched, all new.** No existing method was modified, so every `changed_lines` on pre-existing functions is zero \u2014 no method or class grew.\n- **The new methods are tiny and self-contained.** Each has low CC, and `WMC_other` is bounded by this small new class rather than a large controller/service/mapper. Folding the check into `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` or `ClinicServiceImpl.saveOwner` would have paid for those whole classes' surrounding complexity.\n- I deliberately did **not** add a repository `findByEmail` query \u2014 that would have touched the `OwnerRepository` interface plus its multiple profile implementations (JPA/JDBC/SpringData), multiplying the file count. Reusing `findAllOwners()` matches how the existing telephone/city-capacity rules work.",
